  "summary": "Four preprints from late November 2026 apply targeted computational or engineered fixes to specific acquisition bottlenecks in optical imaging.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these four preprints is the recovery of optical information from measurements that are imperfect or resource-limited, addressed either by computational processing or by engineered acquisition. In single-pixel imaging, a unified calibration and composite-readout framework treats temporal mixing as a measurement resource rather than a nuisance. Event-camera Fourier-transform hyperspectral microscopy uses continuous birefringent interferometer scanning to reach real-time widefield spectral acquisition. MCP-based EUV ptychography applies physics-simulation-trained residual learning to restore diffraction patterns, reporting an edge-response width of about 1.08 μm at 57 nm. The fourth item, a surrogate-guided design of incoherent multispectral diffractive optical networks for onboard satellite image classification, is design-oriented rather than a reconstruction method. What is new in each item is a targeted treatment of a specific error source or resource bottleneck, although the modalities differ considerably.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
